== 2.7 Chapter 2 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [“According to the passage” introduces a question that can be answered by],
  [finding the one sentence that states it],
  [the writer's tone],
  [taking one step from the text],
  [reading the whole passage again]
)

#mcq(2, [The question “The word *table* as used in the passage means…” is answered by],
  [the dictionary sense you learned first],
  [the clause around the word],
  [the tense of the sentence],
  [the title of the passage]
)

#mcq(3, [“The council raised the levy without consulting a trader, and this is what the association objected to.” The referent of *this* is],
  [the raising of the levy without consultation],
  [the traders],
  [the levy],
  [the council]
)

#mcq(4, [A question containing EXCEPT is answered by],
  [the statement that is in the passage],
  [the writer's opinion],
  [the statement that is not in the passage],
  [the longest option]
)

#mcq(5, [Which question type is answered by an adjective rather than a sentence?],
  [negation],
  [reference],
  [tone],
  [detail]
)

#mcq(6, [“The writer's purpose is to…” is best answered with],
  [a noun phrase],
  [a date],
  [a verb such as *warn* or *correct*],
  [a quotation]
)

#mcq(7, [An option that repeats a sentence from the passage word for word is usually],
  [a lifted detail and therefore a trap],
  [the key],
  [an inference],
  [a summary]
)

#mcq(8, [The option that changes one word of a passage sentence — *most* for *all*, *may* for *will* — is called],
  [a restatement],
  [an almost-true],
  [an invention],
  [a reversal]
)

#mcq(9, [A reference question and an inference question can both use the word *it*. The difference is that a reference question],
  [asks what the writer thinks],
  [asks for a quotation],
  [asks for the main idea],
  [asks what the pronoun stands for]
)

#mcq(10, [“Which of the following is FALSE?” requires you to find],
  [the three statements the passage makes],
  [the one statement the passage contradicts or never makes],
  [the writer's conclusion],
  [the main idea]
)

#mcq(11, [A candidate who finds two options that both seem absent from the passage, on a NOT question, should],
  [leave it blank],
  [choose the first one],
  [choose the longer one],
  [return to the passage and check the sentence for each]
)

#mcq(12, [“The writer implies that the scheme will fail.” An implication is],
  [a conclusion the passage's statements force],
  [a fact from outside the passage],
  [the writer's opinion of the reader],
  [a statement the passage makes]
)

#mcq(13, [The commonest wrong option on a comprehension paper is],
  [a very short option],
  [an option that repeats the question],
  [a statement from outside the passage],
  [a true detail that answers a different question]
)

#mcq(14, [“The word *fine* as used in line 3 means…” A candidate who answers *a sum of money paid as a penalty* without reading line 3 is],
  [answering from memory, which the examiner is counting on],
  [using the reference rule],
  [applying the negation rule],
  [using the sentence correctly]
)

#mcq(15, [A vocabulary-in-context answer must],
  [be a synonym from the dictionary],
  [be the word's commonest meaning],
  [fit the sentence and keep its sense],
  [be a single word]
)

#mcq(16, [“What does *they* refer to?” is answered by looking],
  [at the title],
  [forward in the passage],
  [at the writer's tone],
  [back for a plural noun that fits]
)

#mcq(17, [A negation question rewards a candidate who],
  [reads the passage twice],
  [marks the negative word before reading the options],
  [reads the options twice],
  [answers last]
)

#mcq(18, [“The writer's attitude towards the council is best described as…” The answer is found in],
  [the number of quotations],
  [the facts the passage reports],
  [the length of the sentences],
  [the adjectives and verbs the writer chooses]
)

#mcq(19, [The four shapes of wrong option are the lifted detail, the almost-true, the invention and],
  [the reversal],
  [the summary],
  [the quotation],
  [the paraphrase]
)

#mcq(20, [“In line 12, *the proposal* refers to…” The line number is useful because it],
  [narrows the search to one sentence],
  [tells you the answer's length],
  [tells you the passage is difficult],
  [shows the writer's tone]
)

#mcq(21, [An inference question's answer must be],
  [suggested by the passage],
  [stated in the passage],
  [true in the world],
  [the writer's opinion]
)

#mcq(22, [“Which of the following best expresses the main idea?” An option that describes only the third paragraph is],
  [a reversal],
  [too narrow],
  [the key],
  [too broad]
)

#mcq(23, [The question “The passage suggests that the mill will close” is best tested by asking],
  [how long the passage is],
  [what the writer's tone is],
  [which sentence forces that conclusion],
  [whether the mill is mentioned]
)

#mcq(24, [When a question says “the writer's purpose”, the answer is usually found],
  [where the passage stops reporting and starts judging],
  [in the middle paragraph],
  [in the title],
  [in the longest sentence]
)

#mcq(25, [“The word *sanction* as used in the passage means…” A candidate who knows only that *sanction* means *penalise* will],
  [answer the word's other life],
  [read the passage twice],
  [answer correctly],
  [choose the longest option]
)

#mcq(26, [A detail question and a main-idea question differ in that a detail question],
  [wants a verb],
  [wants an adjective],
  [wants one stated fact],
  [wants a claim]
)

#mcq(27, [“The passage implies that the traders were not consulted.” The word *implies* tells you the answer is],
  [a conclusion from what the passage says],
  [quoted in the passage],
  [the writer's opinion of the traders],
  [the main idea]
)

#mcq(28, [A candidate who answers a NOT question with the first option that sounds familiar is],
  [using the reference test],
  [likely choosing a statement that is in the passage],
  [applying the negation rule],
  [checking the answer]
)

#mcq(29, [“What is the meaning of *blame* in the sentence ‘The contractor blames the price of diesel’?” The answer should be],
  [a synonym that fits the clause],
  [a definition copied from a dictionary],
  [the word's etymology],
  [the opposite of *praise*]
)

#mcq(30, [Which question type is answered from the whole passage rather than from one sentence?],
  [vocabulary],
  [reference],
  [main idea],
  [detail]
)

#mcq(31, [“The writer's tone in the last paragraph is…” Tone questions are rare on the paper because],
  [passages are neutral],
  [most passages carry their attitude in a single sentence],
  [tone is not examinable],
  [tone is hard to define]
)

#mcq(32, [The question “Which of the following is NOT true according to the passage?” is best worked],
  [from the title],
  [from the options to the passage],
  [from the passage to the options],
  [from the writer's tone]
)

#mcq(33, [“The new bins have been used, but only by the streets that already had a collector.” The word *but* signals that the sentence],
  [introduces a quotation],
  [limits a claim the council would like to make],
  [repeats the previous sentence],
  [supports the council]
)

#mcq(34, [An option built from a true sentence with its cause and effect swapped is],
  [an invention],
  [a paraphrase],
  [the key],
  [a reversal]
)

#mcq(35, [The safest order for a comprehension passage is],
  [passage, questions, options],
  [options, passage, questions],
  [questions, options, passage, answer],
  [passage, options, questions]
)

#mcq(36, [A question that quotes a phrase in quotation marks tells you],
  [the answer is a quotation],
  [the passage is about language],
  [the phrase is misspelt],
  [the phrase appears in the passage and can be scanned for]
)

#mcq(37, [“The passage suggests that the survey took two years.” The word *suggests* means the answer is],
  [the main idea],
  [supported but not stated],
  [stated word for word],
  [the writer's attitude]
)

#mcq(38, [Which is the best reason for reading all four options before choosing?],
  [the options are alphabetical],
  [a later option may fit the sentence better than an early one],
  [the longest is often right],
  [the key is usually last]
)

#mcq(39, [The final check on any comprehension answer is to],
  [count the words],
  [compare the options with each other],
  [put the option back into the sentence and read it],
  [re-read the whole passage]
)

#mcq(40, [“The passage suggests that the survey took two years.” A candidate who answers *the survey was slow* has],
  [answered a main-idea question],
  [misread the number],
  [added a judgement the passage does not commit itself to],
  [taken the step the passage takes]
)

#shortcut-box("Before you turn to the key")[
  + Mark the whole set in *one pass* — every answer, right or wrong — before you read a single solution.
  + Write the score at the top of the page, because a drill you cannot compare with the last one tells you nothing.
  + Then read *every* solution, including the ones you got right: the second method is in the solution, not in the chapter.
]

